<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/057V">Remark <span data-tag="057V">4.7.7.17</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a simplicial subset which is also an $\infty $-category. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an $n$-category for some integer $n \geq -1$, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ is also an $n$-category. For $n \geq 1$, this follows from Remark <a data-tag="057F" href="/tag/057F">4.7.7.3</a>. The case $n = 0$ follows from Proposition <a data-tag="057U" href="/tag/057U">4.7.7.16</a> (since any subcategory of a partially ordered set is also a partially ordered set), and the case $n = -1$ is trivial (see Example <a data-tag="057N" href="/tag/057N">4.7.7.10</a>). </p>
